IRA Ticket: [FE/BE] Omnibox Chat Interface: Contextual Predefined Actions & LLM Intent Routing
📝 User Story
As a User looking at a data-dense page,

I want to trigger contextual actions (like generating commentary or summaries) either by clicking a predefined button or by typing my request in natural language,

So that I can speed up my workflow using my preferred interaction method without losing conversational continuity.

📋 Background / Context
The application top bar features a unified Chat/Action interface ("Omnibox"). Depending on the active page context, specific "Quick Action" buttons (chips) are rendered (e.g., Generate Commentary for Selected Rows).
To optimize performance and user experience, clicking a button should trigger the downstream business logic immediately (bypassing LLM interpretation), while typing a conversational equivalent should leverage LLM Function Calling to trigger the exact same downstream logic.

⚙️ Technical Approach & Routing Logic
Lane A (Direct Action UI Click):

User clicks a contextual button chip.

Frontend bypasses LLM text classification entirely.

Frontend dispatches a direct API payload to the execution gateway containing the action_id and active page/row state payload.

Lane B (Conversational Text Input):

User types natural language text into the chat bar.

Backend dispatches text to the LLM agent.

The LLM agent is provisioned with system tools matching the available page actions (e.g., generate_commentary()).

If the LLM triggers the function/tool call, the backend routes execution to the identical service used in Lane A.

✅ Acceptance Criteria
1. UI/UX & Interaction
[ ] Contextual Chips: Predefined action chips must render dynamically based on the current page context and state (e.g., "Generate Commentary" appears when rows are selected, or shows a validation state if missing).

[ ] Chat History Injection: When a predefined button chip is clicked, a visual message indicator must be injected into the chat timeline (e.g., "System Action: Generating Commentary...") to preserve conversational context.

[ ] Loading States: The input field and action chips must enter a loading/disabled state while an action is processing to prevent double-submitting.

2. Functional & Routing
[ ] Direct Execution (Bypass Lane): Verification that clicking an action button invokes the target service without calling the LLM orchestrator endpoint, minimizing latency.

[ ] Natural Language Parsing: Typing variations of the action (e.g., "help me write commentary for selected row", "summarize these rows") must successfully resolve to the correct backend function call via the LLM agent.

[ ] State Payload Delivery: Both paths (button click and LLM tool call) must feed the exact same data schema (active page_id, selected_row_ids, and filter states) to the processing engine.

[ ] Graceful Error Handling: If an action is triggered (via button or text) but mandatory state criteria are not met (e.g., no rows selected), the system must handle it gracefully:

Button path: Explicit UI banner warning.

Text path: LLM responds conversationally asking the user to select rows.

🧪 Out of Scope (For This Ticket)
Enhancing or changing the underlying commentary/summary generation algorithm itself.

Persistent storage of the chat history across browser sessions (handled in a separate state-persistence ticket).
